  "yearNarratives": {
    "2015": "Oil prices collapsed to about $52, opening a multibillion-dollar budget gap. Still, population peaked near 738k, the PFD paid $2,072, and a run of record-warm years began.",
    "2016": "Alaska’s warmest year on record (+5.1°F). Recession set in: jobs fell, unemployment hit 6.9%, and the PFD was cut roughly in half to $1,022. The salmon harvest crashed to 113M fish.",
    "2017": "The recession deepened. Net out-migration reached ~7.7k as jobs slid to 333k. Oil output briefly ticked up and the salmon harvest rebounded to 225M.",
    "2018": "A fourth straight year of population loss, with out-migration peaking near 9.8k. Oil recovered to ~$71, and Permanent Fund earnings formally began funding state government.",
    "2019": "The recession ended as jobs stabilized. A July heat wave brought Anchorage its first 90°F day, and tourism hit a then-record ~2.4M visitors.",
    "2020": "The pandemic erased the cruise season — visitors fell ~80%. Unemployment spiked to 8.3% and oil sank to $42. The Census counted 733,391 Alaskans.",
    "2021": "Partial recovery. Independent travelers returned, jobs rebuilt to 315k, and net migration came closest to even in a decade. Salmon rebounded to 234M.",
    "2022": "Oil spiked near $99, funding a record $3,284 PFD including energy relief. Unemployment fell to a historic low of 4.4% in a very tight labor market.",
    "2023": "Cruise tourism set records and visitors topped ~3M. Unemployment hit a new low of 4.3%, but out-migration (~5.9k) resumed as housing and childcare costs squeezed families.",
    "2024": "Population ticked up to ~740k on international arrivals. Oil output held steady as Pikka and Willow advanced, while the salmon harvest fell to ~105M."
  },
